@page "/"

<PageTitle>Algorithm Visualizer — Learn algorithms visually</PageTitle>

<div class="home-page">
    <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-copy">
            <div class="eyebrow"><span>✦</span> Learn the rule, watch the state, explain the change</div>
            <h1>Algorithms make more sense when you can <em>see every decision.</em></h1>
            <p class="hero-lead">
                Build the input yourself, predict what should happen, step through the real C# implementation,
                compare Visual State with Memory State, and finish each lesson with automatically checked practice.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions">
                <a class="primary-action" href="/structures/queue-stack">Start lesson 01 <span>→</span></a>
                <ConceptLink class="secondary-action" Href="/learn/concepts/visual-vs-memory">Concepts &amp; Memory</ConceptLink>
                <a class="secondary-action" href="#learning-path">See all 24 modules</a>
            </div>
            <div class="hero-note">
                <span class="note-icon">i</span>
                <span><strong>Best way to use the app:</strong> predict the next step first, then use Step and the explanation to verify your reasoning.</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-visual" aria-label="Algorithm learning preview">
            <div class="visual-card">
                <div class="visual-card-top">
                    <div>
                        <span class="mini-label">ONE ACTION · THREE VIEWS</span>
                        <strong>Stack · PUSH(8)</strong>
                    </div>
                    <span class="live-pill"><i></i> Interactive</span>
                </div>

                <div class="stack-preview">
                    <div class="preview-pointer">TOP →</div>
                    <div class="preview-block active">8</div>
                    <div class="preview-block">5</div>
                    <div class="preview-block">2</div>
                </div>

                <div class="preview-explanation">
                    <span class="step-number">03</span>
                    <div>
                        <small>WHY DID THIS CHANGE?</small>
                        <p><strong>8</strong> became the new top because Stack follows <strong>LIFO</strong>. Memory State keeps its own element identity even if another item also has value 8.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <span class="floating-chip chip-one">Visual State</span>
            <span class="floating-chip chip-two">Memory State</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="quick-stats" aria-label="Implemented learning modules">
        <div><strong>24</strong><span>interactive modules</span></div>
        <div><strong>9</strong><span>data-structure labs</span></div>
        <div><strong>13</strong><span>classical algorithm labs</span></div>
        <div><strong>2</strong><span>live ML foundation labs</span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="foundation-callout" aria-labelledby="foundation-title">
        <div class="foundation-copy">
            <span class="section-kicker">LEARN FIRST</span>
            <h2 id="foundation-title">Use Concepts &amp; Memory when a label is unclear.</h2>
            <p>It is the shared reference for Visual vs Memory State, identity, Big-O, tree height, heap indexing, matrix storage, graph adjacency, every implemented sorting strategy, Linear/Binary Search, graph traversal with BFS/DFS, weighted shortest paths with Dijkstra, dependency order, minimum spanning trees with Prim/Kruskal, plus Vector fundamentals, Gradient Descent, and the live Linear Regression training model.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-links">
            <ConceptLink Href="/learn/concepts/visual-vs-memory"><strong>Visual vs memory</strong><span>What the picture shows vs what is stored →</span></ConceptLink>
            <ConceptLink Href="/learn/concepts/identity"><strong>Value vs ID</strong><span>Why equal values can be different elements →</span></ConceptLink>
            <ConceptLink Href="/learn/concepts/big-o"><strong>Big-O</strong><span>Read O(1), O(n), Θ(n²), Θ(n log n) →</span></ConceptLink>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learning-section" id="learning-path">
